id: pm-gh1699
title: "GH-1699: secret guard recognises quoted keys, nested JSON values and XML elements"
description: "GitHub issue 1699 (reported against 2026.11.10): scanMutationSecrets and the CLI mutation guard flag password: value and password=value but miss the same credential when the key is quoted. JSON, Python-dict and XML spellings get no finding, and neither does a credential nested in an array or object under the key. Reproduced on main with the built SDK: {\"password\":\"<8+ chars>\"}, 'password':'<8+ chars>', a nested array, a nested object and <password>...</password> all return []. The high_entropy_assignment rule has the same gap for quoted keys. Root cause: both assignment regexes require the separator to follow the key word directly (\\b(?:password|passphrase)\\s*[:=]), so the key's closing quote defeats them, and neither rule descends into containers. The same SECRET_RULES inventory feeds the repository secret scan (scripts/check-secrets-lib.mjs), so a widened rule must stay clean over all tracked files. Extensions (pm-memory) currently hand-write mixed-content parsers to close these gaps; one structural rule in the SDK replaces them. Duplicate check: searched 'secret guard quoted keys', 'secret', 'credential redact' (open + closed). pm-pim7 (closed) introduced the guard and pm-fhhhlk (closed) added npm/Slack shapes; neither covered key quoting or nesting."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: agent-ux,"gh:1699",sdk,secret-guard,security
created_at: "2026-11-10T12:43:16.829Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-10T18:43:51.476Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-10T14:35:36.829Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-10T14:35:36.829Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 150
acceptance_criteria: "A must-refuse corpus covers the quoted-key (JSON, single-quoted), nested-array, nested-object and XML-element forms from GH-1699 for password_assignment, plus quoted-key high_entropy_assignment; A must-allow corpus keeps \"password\": \"\", \"password\": \"${BUILD_PASSWORD}\", \"password_policy\": \"rotate monthly\", placeholder container tokens such as password=[scrubbed], and prose such as 'the password field' finding-free; The CLI guard (built CLI, real create) and scanMutationSecrets return the same findings for both corpora, and the repository scan reports the same rule for the shared corpus; The structural pass is linear: a pathological input (deep nesting, unterminated strings, many repeated keys) completes within a bounded-time control; node scripts/check-secrets.mjs stays clean over every tracked file"
parent: pm-qwoy
risk: medium
confidence: high
resolution: "secret-rules.ts declares a SecretStructuredSpelling on password_assignment (with element content) and high_entropy_assignment. The flat regexes are composed from KEY + VALUE constants and stay byte-identical to main. A linear structured pass judges a quoted key's scalar value and every quoted string in value position inside arrays/objects with the rule's own sticky value grammar. Structured values may not span a raw line break, and bare container tokens are never judged. The key cursor is cloned per scan."
expected_result: "The GH-1699 must-refuse corpus is detected and the must-allow corpus is clean in the SDK scanner, the CLI guard and the repository scan; check-secrets stays clean; pathological inputs stay linear."
actual_result: "secret-policy-parity: 83 tests pass, covering 16 refuse and 18 allow spellings in both consumers, high-entropy quoted keys, re-entrancy and a 9-input pathological control. A real create through the built CLI matches scanMutationSecrets for both corpora. node scripts/check-secrets.mjs is clean over all tracked files. The structured pass costs about 190 ms per 196 MB scan."
